e work, ranking 14th in EPA allowed while the offense sits 23rd in EPA per play. What sets them apart is the 2nd highest screen rate in the league.","offense":"The Falcons offense has been well below average by EPA per play so far, and it has been much better running the ball than passing it. They have been one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the 2nd highest screen rate in the league. Michael Penix Jr. has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Drake London, who has drawn 29% of the targets.","defense":"The Falcons defense has been a little above average by EPA allowed per play so far. Its strongest area has been explosive play rate allowed, where it ranks 11th, and its weakest has been sack rate, where it ranks 21st. Za'Darius Smith has led the way with 4.5 sacks. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of pass rushers that ranks 5th lowest in the league.","special":"The Falcons have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 80% of field goals, which is a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the offensive line, about 29% of the money on the books. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Michael Penix Jr. has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,157 yards. Drake London has been the clear top target with 29% of the looks and 616 receiving yards. Bijan Robinson has handled most of the work in the backfield with 69% of the carries. On defense, Za'Darius Smith leads the team with 4.5 sacks.","coaching":"Kevin Stefanski is calling the offense as head coach and Jeff Ulbrich is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2022 Panthers and the 2023 Broncos under Sean Payton. Their defense looks most like the 2023 Saints under Dennis Allen and the 2023 49ers.","trends":"The offense went from 24th in EPA per play in 2025 to 23rd so far this season, about where it was. The defense moved from 15th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 14th so far this season, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was blitz rate, which went from 35% to 25%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}